WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.500
Everyone in this photo died soon after it
was taken.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:09.550
These are British explorers standing at the
South Pole in January 1912.

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:13.000
The photo marks the finish line of a race
into the unknown.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.940
Two teams, one British, one Norwegian, trekked
900 miles into brutal territory and had to

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.300
get back to safety before winter hit.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.000
And at first glance, this looks like a victory
photo for the British.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.760
Except that is the Norwegian flag.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.740
And it only gets worse from here.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.180
Robert Falcon Scott was a meticulous planner.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:45.280
And his dream was to be the first person to
reach the South Pole.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:50.420
He and his English team of explorers and scientists
had been conducting research in Antarctica

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.049
and collected years of data on seasonal cycles
on the continent.

00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:58.019
These lines show what they estimated average
temperatures would be throughout the year,

00:00:58.019 --> 00:01:03.190
with summer ranging from around 30 to negative
10 degrees Fahrenheit, and huge drops beginning

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.519
around April.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:09.079
Remember this chart because later, it will
help us understand Scott’s decision-making.

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:14.149
Scott planned to use pony transport for the
first 425 miles across the Ross Ice Shelf,

00:01:14.149 --> 00:01:18.560
shoot them at the base of the Beardmore Glacier,
and finish the rest of the journey on foot.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:23.870
Which included a 125-mile hike across the
top of the glacier, 350 more miles to the

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:29.330
pole, and all the way back again, all while
hauling hundreds of pounds of equipment.

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:33.460
Using ponies and brute strength made sense
to Scott at the time:

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.090
British explorers had used this method to
haul equipment during an earlier attempt on

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:38.090
the South Pole.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.119
Plus, the English didn’t have experience
with the other good option: dog teams.

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:45.939
And they believed man-hauling was the surest
way to make the tricky climb up the glacier

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:49.450
and on to the Polar Plateau, where the South
Pole sits.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.600
It was hard, slow work, but the route they
were on had reached the plateau before, and

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.639
it seemed to be worth the effort.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.020
But Scott’s team wasn’t alone.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.740
Norwegian explorer Roald Amundsen was camped
nearby.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.509
And he wanted to get to the pole first.

00:02:06.509 --> 00:02:11.760
The Norwegian team, all of them expert skiers,
knew how to travel in cold conditions.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.340
And to make matters worse for the English,
Amundsen had dogs — and he knew how to use them.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.500
News of the Norwegians’ last-minute bid
worried Scott, but he was still optimistic.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:25.220
Amundsen had started about 60 miles closer
to the pole but was taking a route not yet

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:27.120
proven to be passable.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.880
Coming against an unknown obstacle

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:33.080
or falling into an unmarked crevasse could end his attempt prematurely.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:35.360
But that’s not how it happened.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.340
By the time Scott reached his goal, Amundsen’s
flag was there waiting for him.

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:44.530
The Norwegians and their dogs had comfortably
reached the pole five weeks earlier

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.940
and were almost back to their starting point
by the time the English arrived.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.760
Scott and his team were heartbroken.

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.000
They took this photo outside of Amundsen’s
tent the day they started their long journey back.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.600
Scott wrote:

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.590
Left a note to say I had visited the tent
with companions.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:01.930
Bowers photographing and Wilson sketching.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:06.549
We have turned our back now on the goal of
our ambition and must face our 800 miles of

00:03:06.549 --> 00:03:10.300
solid dragging — and good-bye to most of
the daydreams!

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.530
This is where the trouble really begins.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:17.590
It’s mid-January in this photo —

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.680
still the height of the Antarctic summer.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.230
Told you this chart was coming back.

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:24.760
According to their research, the team had
about 3 months left before temperatures on

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:29.439
the Ross Ice Shelf, the last leg of the journey
back, would drop to deadly levels.

00:03:29.439 --> 00:03:32.620
That left plenty of time to make the long
trek on foot.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.490
But this isn’t what happened in 1912.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:39.520
This is that average line again, and these
are the temperatures Scott’s party endured

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.110
that summer: consecutive days of temperatures
around minus 40 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:48.520
Those conditions, at prolonged exposure, are
not survivable.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.260
The first man died here — he collapsed and
soon went comatose following several falls

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:55.260
on the glacier.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.390
The next man died about a month later, after
crippling frostbite in his hands and feet

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:03.040
began hurting the team’s progress and their
chances of survival.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.660
Nearly unable to walk, he left the tent and
sacrificed himself to a snowstorm.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:12.760
The last three, including Scott, made it here
before getting trapped in their tent by a blizzard,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:17.160
just 11 miles from the supply depot that would
have saved their lives.

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:26.980
The tent, along with the bodies, journals,
and photographs, was found 8 months later

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.120
by a search team.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:33.280
As time went on, Scott’s legacy vacillated
between fearless explorer and bumbling fool

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.160
who tried to take ponies into the Antarctic.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.020
But the thing is his plan should have worked.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.650
Measurements from modern weather stations
along his route show the predictions he was

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.000
relying on were impressively accurate.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.690
What Scott couldn’t have known is that 1912
was an anomaly — the temperatures his party

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:54.660
suffered through occur roughly once every
15 years, turning an already risky venture

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.700
into a hopeless one.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.660
The photo they took outside of Amundsen’s
tent was meant to be a gentlemanly admission

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:03.110
of defeat at the end of a long race.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.120
But instead, it was the starting line of a
race they didn't see coming — a desperate

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.400
attempt to escape from the coldest place on
Earth.

